orophytum_comosum.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:Redirect/page/15368539","extmetadata":{"DateTime":{"value":"2011-06-01 09:21:19","source":"mediawiki-metadata","hidden":""},"ObjectName":{"value":"Root-bound Chlorophytum comosum","source":"mediawiki-metadata"},"CommonsMetadataExtension":{"value":1.2,"source":"extension","hidden":""},"Categories":{"value":"Chlorophytum comosum (cultivars)|Taken with Canon PowerShot S90|Repotting|Flickr images uploaded by Flickr upload bot|Roots of potted plants","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Assessments":{"value":"","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"ImageDescription":{"value":"This is a <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/chlorophytum_comosum\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:chlorophytum comosum\">chlorophytum comosum</a> or spider plant to you and me. As you can see it desperately needed re-potting. \n<p>In spite of this tight root ball, the  plant is healthy, lives outside and has sent out numerous runners which have rooted in the soil. It even has flowers which indicate it is a mature plant. \n</p>\nThanks to my timely intervention, it now lives in a larger pot which I dare say it will outgrow in a year's time. (original text)","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2010-05-23 16:34:17","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Flickr\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Flickr\">Flickr</a>: <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/34673186@N03/4634196989\">The root of the problem Project 365(2) Day 111</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/people/34673186@N03\">Keith Williamson</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Permission":{"value":"<table id=\"FlickrReview\" style=\"width:100%; margin:0.3em auto; text-align:start; background-color:var(--background-color-success-subtle,#d5fdf4);